WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.358 --> 00:00:04.221
- What's up Believe Nation?

00:00:04.221 --> 00:00:06.421
It's Evan, my one word is believe

00:00:06.421 --> 00:00:08.848
and I believe that you
have the ability to create

00:00:08.848 --> 00:00:11.409
something special that
will change the planet.

00:00:11.409 --> 00:00:12.700
To help you on your journey,

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.305
I started the Mentor Me series,

00:00:14.305 --> 00:00:16.285
and the goal here is to
try to hang around people

00:00:16.285 --> 00:00:17.934
who have done a lot more than us,

00:00:17.934 --> 00:00:19.925
who have achieved massive success,

00:00:19.925 --> 00:00:22.417
and hopefully by hanging around
theme a little bit longer,

00:00:22.417 --> 00:00:24.916
some of their mindsets, their
attitudes, their beliefs,

00:00:24.916 --> 00:00:27.527
the way they see the
world, will seep into us,

00:00:27.527 --> 00:00:30.529
to help us become the
best version of ourselves.

00:00:30.529 --> 00:00:32.319
So, today we're going to
learn from Brene Brown,

00:00:32.319 --> 00:00:35.421
and the power of
vulnerability and emotions.

00:00:35.421 --> 00:00:36.579
Mentor me, Brene.

00:00:36.579 --> 00:00:38.561
Rule number three is my personal favorite,

00:00:38.561 --> 00:00:41.926
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:41.926 --> 00:00:44.509
(upbeat music)

00:00:54.018 --> 00:00:57.742
- I want to start with two questions,

00:00:57.742 --> 00:00:59.177
that I really want you to think about.

00:00:59.177 --> 00:01:02.015
And the two questions are,

00:01:02.015 --> 00:01:04.765
what should I be afraid of today?

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:08.387
And, who's to blame?

00:01:14.159 --> 00:01:17.743
What should scare me
and who's fault is it?

00:01:17.743 --> 00:01:21.910
To me, these are the most
profoundly dangerous questions

00:01:24.337 --> 00:01:27.004
that we center our lives around.

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:32.033
And, to me, what they are indicators of,

00:01:32.033 --> 00:01:36.200
is a culture of scarcity,
a culture of not enough.

00:01:37.118 --> 00:01:39.788
And when you think about
any milieu in your life,

00:01:39.788 --> 00:01:41.714
think about education.

00:01:41.714 --> 00:01:43.908
What are we supposed to be afraid of,

00:01:43.908 --> 00:01:45.614
and who's to blame?

00:01:45.614 --> 00:01:48.697
Politics, the economy, social issues.

00:01:50.621 --> 00:01:51.949
I guarantee you if you turn on the news

00:01:51.949 --> 00:01:55.607
or opened a paper today,
the focus would be

00:01:55.607 --> 00:01:56.850
here's why you should be afraid

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.254
and here's who's fault it is.

00:01:59.254 --> 00:02:01.842
What's interesting to me about that,

00:02:01.842 --> 00:02:04.950
is it's not just at a cultural
level, or a community level,

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:08.533
that stuff happens in
my house, every week.

00:02:09.612 --> 00:02:11.487
Every week, with my husband,

00:02:11.487 --> 00:02:14.736
I talk to him about why
we should be fearful

00:02:14.736 --> 00:02:16.147
and who's fault it is.

00:02:16.147 --> 00:02:17.810
Mostly it's his.

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:19.528
(laughing)

00:02:19.528 --> 00:02:21.207
But you know, if something doesn't go well

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:24.244
at my daughter's school,
then I'm like, oh my God,

00:02:24.244 --> 00:02:28.411
this is going to have huge
implications for university,

00:02:30.331 --> 00:02:31.825
and we should be terrified right now

00:02:31.825 --> 00:02:32.972
that she's not getting cursive.

00:02:32.972 --> 00:02:34.108
I know she's four,

00:02:34.108 --> 00:02:36.155
(laughing)

00:02:36.155 --> 00:02:37.990
but this is still relevant.

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:39.337
It's indicative of scarcity.

00:02:39.337 --> 00:02:42.390
So let's talk about
what scarcity culture is

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:43.223
and what it looks like.

00:02:43.223 --> 00:02:45.676
Scarcity culture is best understood

00:02:45.676 --> 00:02:49.257
with the simple question of never enough.

00:02:49.257 --> 00:02:51.291
And you can fill in the blanks,

00:02:51.291 --> 00:02:54.287
never good enough, safe
enough, certain enough,

00:02:54.287 --> 00:02:57.370
perfect enough, relevant
enough, and perhaps, I think,

00:02:57.370 --> 00:03:01.537
one of the most dangerous
and insidious messages today,

00:03:02.406 --> 00:03:04.752
is never extraordinary enough.

00:03:04.752 --> 00:03:09.661
Somehow, in the world we live
in today, an ordinary life

00:03:09.661 --> 00:03:13.494
has become synonymous
with a meaningless life.

00:03:15.202 --> 00:03:19.714
I've watched it happen,
not only as a researcher,

00:03:19.714 --> 00:03:20.997
I'm a qualitative researcher,

00:03:20.997 --> 00:03:23.436
so I sit across from people
and look in their eyes

00:03:23.436 --> 00:03:26.583
and I have a good sense of
what's going on in their lives.

00:03:26.583 --> 00:03:27.657
But, as a university professor,

00:03:27.657 --> 00:03:31.159
who's been teaching for the last 17 years,

00:03:31.159 --> 00:03:34.460
it's been very clear to
me that extraordinary

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:35.710
means relevant.

00:03:37.167 --> 00:03:40.300
And the top one is the big
one, for all of us personally,

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:41.800
never good enough.

00:03:42.875 --> 00:03:46.111
Which is shame, which we
all have, on occasion,

00:03:46.111 --> 00:03:49.323
that sense that we're not
smart enough, good enough,

00:03:49.323 --> 00:03:52.089
perfect enough, loved
enough, promoted enough,

00:03:52.089 --> 00:03:54.047
we're not enough.

00:03:54.047 --> 00:03:56.247
So what we do in response
to scarcity culture,

00:03:56.247 --> 00:03:59.330
I think all of us every day, is this.

00:04:02.084 --> 00:04:04.844
We wake up in the morning and we armor up.

00:04:04.844 --> 00:04:07.884
And we put it on and we say, I'm
going to go out in the world,

00:04:07.884 --> 00:04:11.359
I'm basically going to just kick some ass,

00:04:11.359 --> 00:04:13.898
I'm not going to let anyone see who I am,

00:04:13.898 --> 00:04:16.659
and in doing so, I can protect myself,

00:04:16.659 --> 00:04:18.855
against the things that hurt the most,

00:04:18.855 --> 00:04:23.056
judgment, criticism,
fear, blame, ridicule.

00:04:23.056 --> 00:04:25.880
I'm going to armor up
and I'm going to be safe.

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:28.166
And we rationalize and
we convince ourselves

00:04:28.166 --> 00:04:30.833
that this armor is really smart.

00:04:32.009 --> 00:04:34.481
And we use a lot of different
things to armor, too.

00:04:34.481 --> 00:04:37.231
Perfectionism, intellectualizing,

00:04:39.086 --> 00:04:42.416
but if you get really, really, clear

00:04:42.416 --> 00:04:43.895
about what we're doing every morning

00:04:43.895 --> 00:04:48.062
when we wake up and decide
to armor, it's this simple.

00:04:49.864 --> 00:04:52.364
This is what we're protecting,

00:04:53.302 --> 00:04:57.469
We're protecting being hurt,
for a very simple reason,

00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:02.373
that no one really wants to
talk about, which is this,

00:05:02.373 --> 00:05:05.833
love, belonging, irreducible needs

00:05:05.833 --> 00:05:07.829
of men, women, and children.

00:05:07.829 --> 00:05:10.983
In the absence of love and belonging,

00:05:10.983 --> 00:05:13.096
there's always suffering.

00:05:13.096 --> 00:05:16.188
And so what we believe is
that we can protect our sense

00:05:16.188 --> 00:05:19.632
of being lovable and being acceptable,

00:05:19.632 --> 00:05:22.799
and being worth connection with armor.

00:05:26.001 --> 00:05:29.582
Here's the very worst
news from my research,

00:05:29.582 --> 00:05:33.394
the news that above all
other, I did not want to hear,

00:05:33.394 --> 00:05:35.558
it's simply this, and I don't
even want to say it to you

00:05:35.558 --> 00:05:38.056
because I think it's mean to say it.

00:05:38.056 --> 00:05:40.889
But it's so true, and it's simply,

00:05:42.203 --> 00:05:44.953
our capacity for wholeheartedness

00:05:47.026 --> 00:05:48.534
can never be greater

00:05:48.534 --> 00:05:52.719
than our willingness to be brokenhearted.

00:05:52.719 --> 00:05:56.719
Which is a terrible thing,
but a truthful thing.

00:05:59.744 --> 00:06:03.193
That we can only love and be loved

00:06:03.193 --> 00:06:07.115
as much as we are willing
to have our heart broken.

00:06:07.115 --> 00:06:10.733
And so when we move through
the world like this,

00:06:10.733 --> 00:06:12.347
and with our partners, with our children,

00:06:12.347 --> 00:06:14.369
with our friends, in classrooms,

00:06:14.369 --> 00:06:15.754
with the people who work for us,

00:06:15.754 --> 00:06:18.912
with the people we work for,

00:06:18.912 --> 00:06:21.939
there are tremendous casualties.

00:06:21.939 --> 00:06:25.307
The biggest myth about
vulnerability is that it's weakness.

00:06:25.307 --> 00:06:28.002
I think a lot of people
were raised to believe that.

00:06:28.002 --> 00:06:32.176
It was modeled, certainly in our culture,

00:06:32.176 --> 00:06:33.658
we see that a lot.

00:06:33.658 --> 00:06:36.711
To be vulnerable, to
be open, to be exposed,

00:06:36.711 --> 00:06:38.333
is to be weak.

00:06:38.333 --> 00:06:40.763
And the truth is, what
I found in my research,

00:06:40.763 --> 00:06:42.612
is that vulnerability is not weakness.

00:06:42.612 --> 00:06:44.642
In fact, I would argue
that it's our greatest

00:06:44.642 --> 00:06:46.008
measure of courage.

00:06:46.008 --> 00:06:48.291
When we went out an asked
people, what is vulnerability,

00:06:48.291 --> 00:06:51.698
we heard things like,
vulnerability is the first date

00:06:51.698 --> 00:06:52.939
after my divorce.

00:06:52.939 --> 00:06:55.173
Vulnerability is starting my own company.

00:06:55.173 --> 00:06:57.207
Vulnerability is taking
responsibility for something

00:06:57.207 --> 00:06:59.213
that went wrong at work.

00:06:59.213 --> 00:07:01.033
Vulnerability is sitting with my wife

00:07:01.033 --> 00:07:02.342
who has stage three breast cancer

00:07:02.342 --> 00:07:05.410
and making plans for our young kids.

00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:09.169
Vulnerability is taking
my business public.

00:07:09.169 --> 00:07:12.515
The definition I use in
my work, of vulnerability,

00:07:12.515 --> 00:07:16.598
is simply uncertainty, risk
and emotional exposure.

00:07:16.598 --> 00:07:19.756
Vulnerability is about the
willingness to show up,

00:07:19.756 --> 00:07:24.709
and to be seen, even when
there are no guarantees.

00:07:24.709 --> 00:07:27.283
It's interesting to me, one
of the things that I thought

00:07:27.283 --> 00:07:29.159
was really interesting, I gave a talk,

00:07:29.159 --> 00:07:31.008
it's probably a couple of years now,

00:07:31.008 --> 00:07:33.531
and it was being
translated by people doing

00:07:33.531 --> 00:07:34.482
American Sign Language.

00:07:34.482 --> 00:07:36.716
They came up before the
talk started and they said,

00:07:36.716 --> 00:07:39.343
are there any words that
you're going to use a lot

00:07:39.343 --> 00:07:41.098
in your talk that we should know about

00:07:41.098 --> 00:07:42.263
that might be different.

00:07:42.263 --> 00:07:43.954
And, I said, well I use the
word vulnerability a lot.

00:07:43.954 --> 00:07:45.870
There were two of them, they
kind of looked at each other

00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:49.311
and they said, oh, we do
this, for vulnerability.

00:07:49.311 --> 00:07:50.473
And I said, what does that mean?

00:07:50.473 --> 00:07:52.032
They said it means weak in the knees.

00:07:52.032 --> 00:07:56.149
I'm like, wow, that's not how
I talk about vulnerability.

00:07:56.149 --> 00:07:59.284
And she said there's only one
other sign for vulnerability.

00:07:59.284 --> 00:08:00.117
And I said, what is it.

00:08:00.117 --> 00:08:01.200
And she said,

00:08:02.336 --> 00:08:04.980
And, I said, oh, that's
what I'm talking about.

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:09.549
And, so, to me, vulnerability
is our most accurate

00:08:09.549 --> 00:08:10.832
measure of courage.

00:08:10.832 --> 00:08:12.100
I mean it's pretty powerful

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:13.579
when I have 13,000 pieces of data

00:08:13.579 --> 00:08:15.086
collected over 12 years,

00:08:15.086 --> 00:08:18.193
that I cannot find a single
incident or story of courage,

00:08:18.193 --> 00:08:22.601
that was not completely
underpinned by vulnerability.

00:08:22.601 --> 00:08:24.634
I think the problem arises that

00:08:24.634 --> 00:08:27.268
there's so many little
paradoxes with vulnerability

00:08:27.268 --> 00:08:31.435
and one of them is that
vulnerability is courage in you,

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:35.445
but weakness in me.

00:08:35.445 --> 00:08:38.039
When I meet you, it's the
first thing I look for in you.

00:08:38.039 --> 00:08:40.543
But it's the last thing
I want to show you in me.

00:08:40.543 --> 00:08:45.271
And, so, I think, to really
put ourselves out there,

00:08:45.271 --> 00:08:48.957
knowing that if we do that
enough, we're going to fail,

00:08:48.957 --> 00:08:51.440
I just don't think it gets
more courageous than that.

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:54.380
If I roughly took the
people I interviewed,

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:56.713
and divided them into people

00:08:57.749 --> 00:09:00.693
who really have a sense of worthiness,

00:09:00.693 --> 00:09:03.609
that's what this comes down
to, a sense of worthiness,

00:09:03.609 --> 00:09:07.933
they have strong sense
of love and belonging,

00:09:07.933 --> 00:09:09.741
and folks who struggle for it,

00:09:09.741 --> 00:09:12.795
and folks who are always
wondering if they're good enough,

00:09:12.795 --> 00:09:15.328
there was only one variable
that separated the people

00:09:15.328 --> 00:09:18.243
who have a strong sense
of love and belonging

00:09:18.243 --> 00:09:21.902
and really struggle for
it, and that was the people

00:09:21.902 --> 00:09:24.596
who have a strong sense
of love and belonging

00:09:24.596 --> 00:09:27.358
believe they're worthy
of love and belonging.

00:09:27.358 --> 00:09:28.191
That's it.

00:09:29.313 --> 00:09:32.456
They believe they're worthy.

00:09:32.456 --> 00:09:36.039
And, to me, the hard
part of, the one thing

00:09:37.184 --> 00:09:38.278
that keeps us out of connection,

00:09:38.278 --> 00:09:41.591
is our fear that we're
not worthy of connection

00:09:41.591 --> 00:09:43.103
was something that personally
and professionally,

00:09:43.103 --> 00:09:46.497
I felt like I needed to understand better.

00:09:46.497 --> 00:09:50.414
So, what I did, is I took
all of the interviews

00:09:51.356 --> 00:09:54.552
where I saw worthiness, where
I saw people living that way,

00:09:54.552 --> 00:09:57.024
and just looked at those.

00:09:57.024 --> 00:09:59.163
What do these people have in common?

00:09:59.163 --> 00:10:01.593
And I have a slight
office supply addiction,

00:10:01.593 --> 00:10:02.780
but that's another talk.

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:04.130
(laughing)

00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:07.835
So, I had a manila folder
and I had a sharpie,

00:10:07.835 --> 00:10:09.514
and I was like, what am I
going to call this research?

00:10:09.514 --> 00:10:13.623
The first words that came to
my mind were wholehearted.

00:10:13.623 --> 00:10:15.971
These are wholehearted
people, living from this deep

00:10:15.971 --> 00:10:16.978
sense of worthiness.

00:10:16.978 --> 00:10:20.041
So, I wrote at the top
of the manila folder,

00:10:20.041 --> 00:10:21.245
and I started looking at the data.

00:10:21.245 --> 00:10:25.412
In fact, I did it at first,
in a four day, very intensive,

00:10:27.101 --> 00:10:29.277
data analysis, where I went
back, pulled these interviews,

00:10:29.277 --> 00:10:30.896
pulled these stories,
pulled the incidents,

00:10:30.896 --> 00:10:34.115
what's the theme, what's the pattern?

00:10:34.115 --> 00:10:35.918
My husband left town with the kids,

00:10:35.918 --> 00:10:37.461
(laughing)

00:10:37.461 --> 00:10:40.115
because I always go into this
Jackson Pollock crazy thing,

00:10:40.115 --> 00:10:44.198
where I'm just writing
and in my researcher mode.

00:10:45.447 --> 00:10:47.780
And so, here's what I found.

00:10:49.135 --> 00:10:52.532
What they had in common,
was a sense of courage.

00:10:52.532 --> 00:10:55.605
And I want to separate courage and bravery

00:10:55.605 --> 00:10:57.137
for you for a minute.

00:10:57.137 --> 00:10:59.868
Courage, the original
definition of courage,

00:10:59.868 --> 00:11:02.024
when it first came into
the English language,

00:11:02.024 --> 00:11:04.111
it's from the Latin
word cor, meaning heart,

00:11:04.111 --> 00:11:05.838
and the original definition
was to tell the story

00:11:05.838 --> 00:11:09.473
of who you are with your whole heart.

00:11:09.473 --> 00:11:11.648
And so these folks had very simply,

00:11:11.648 --> 00:11:13.981
the courage to be imperfect.

00:11:16.151 --> 00:11:19.993
They had the compassion to
be kind to themselves first,

00:11:19.993 --> 00:11:22.121
and then to others,
because as it turns out,

00:11:22.121 --> 00:11:24.007
we can't practice compassion
with other people,

00:11:24.007 --> 00:11:26.716
if we can't treat ourselves kindly.

00:11:26.716 --> 00:11:29.109
And the last was they had connection,

00:11:29.109 --> 00:11:33.993
and this was the hard part,
as a result of authenticity.

00:11:33.993 --> 00:11:36.187
They were willing to let
go of who they thought they

00:11:36.187 --> 00:11:39.006
should be, in order to be who they were,

00:11:39.006 --> 00:11:43.173
which you have to absolutely
do that for connection.

00:11:45.741 --> 00:11:49.824
The other thing that they
had in common was this,

00:11:53.362 --> 00:11:56.195
they fully embraced vulnerability.

00:11:59.993 --> 00:12:03.743
They believed that what
made them vulnerable,

00:12:03.743 --> 00:12:05.410
made them beautiful.

00:12:08.905 --> 00:12:13.446
They didn't talk about
vulnerability being comfortable,

00:12:13.446 --> 00:12:15.154
nor did they really talk
about being excruciating,

00:12:15.154 --> 00:12:17.768
as I had heard it earlier
in the shame interviewing,

00:12:17.768 --> 00:12:21.913
they just talked about it being necessary.

00:12:21.913 --> 00:12:26.841
They talked about the willingness
to say I love you first.

00:12:26.841 --> 00:12:29.424
The willingness to do something

00:12:30.459 --> 00:12:32.959
where there are no guarantees.

00:12:33.869 --> 00:12:37.382
The willingness to breathe
through the waiting

00:12:37.382 --> 00:12:41.174
for the doctor to call
after your mammogram.

00:12:41.174 --> 00:12:44.343
Be willing to invest in a relationship

00:12:44.343 --> 00:12:46.985
that may or may not work out.

00:12:46.985 --> 00:12:48.690
They thought this was fundamental.

00:12:48.690 --> 00:12:50.739
Let me tell you this real
story, this just happened.

00:12:50.739 --> 00:12:53.143
I spoke at HubSpot last week.

00:12:53.143 --> 00:12:56.178
OK, 13,000 in this
Boston convention center.

00:12:56.178 --> 00:12:58.716
It's like 10 minutes before I go on,

00:12:58.716 --> 00:13:02.533
and I look at Twitter and this
person sends this tweet out

00:13:02.533 --> 00:13:05.530
that says, why is Brene at HubSpot?

00:13:05.530 --> 00:13:08.541
Why is @brenebrown at HubSpot 2015?

00:13:08.541 --> 00:13:10.828
And he had just tweeted love Seth Godin,

00:13:10.828 --> 00:13:12.027
love Amy Schumer, all these people but,

00:13:12.027 --> 00:13:13.479
why is Brene Brown?

00:13:13.479 --> 00:13:16.174
And I'm like, oh, my
God, why am I at HubSpot?

00:13:16.174 --> 00:13:17.454
What am I doing here?

00:13:17.454 --> 00:13:18.804
(laughing)

00:13:18.804 --> 00:13:20.676
And the, I start sweating and even though

00:13:20.676 --> 00:13:23.668
it's a convention center,
it's like, ladies,

00:13:23.668 --> 00:13:24.947
and I'm like, oh my God, what am I doing?

00:13:24.947 --> 00:13:28.528
And I'm the opening keynote
and then, he tweets it again.

00:13:28.528 --> 00:13:30.788
And, I'm like, oh my God.

00:13:30.788 --> 00:13:32.542
These are marketing people,
I don't know anything.

00:13:32.542 --> 00:13:36.463
So I start Googling on my
phone, marketing terms 2015.

00:13:36.463 --> 00:13:38.519
(laughing)

00:13:38.519 --> 00:13:39.936
Wait, the first term that
comes up is incentivize,

00:13:39.936 --> 00:13:42.315
I'm like, I've got to
work the word incentivize

00:13:42.315 --> 00:13:43.433
into this keynote.

00:13:43.433 --> 00:13:45.266
I'm like, what does that
mean exactly, I don't know,

00:13:45.266 --> 00:13:46.618
(laughing)

00:13:46.618 --> 00:13:48.531
but I'm going to say, we're
going to incentivize, I'm like,

00:13:48.531 --> 00:13:52.499
what is happening, and I have
a total crisis of confidence.

00:13:52.499 --> 00:13:55.061
Shame drives two tapes, not good enough

00:13:55.061 --> 00:13:56.764
and who do you think you are?

00:13:56.764 --> 00:13:58.919
I'm like, who, who am I?

00:13:58.919 --> 00:14:01.571
Right, snaps, terrible.

00:14:01.571 --> 00:14:04.979
Then all of a sudden, I'm like, who is it?

00:14:04.979 --> 00:14:07.225
Because what happens when
you get backed into a corner?

00:14:07.225 --> 00:14:08.655
My brain is making up this story,

00:14:08.655 --> 00:14:10.119
you don't belong here.

00:14:10.119 --> 00:14:11.611
I'm like that story is not going to work

00:14:11.611 --> 00:14:12.844
because I'm going on in five seconds.

00:14:12.844 --> 00:14:13.847
(laughing)

00:14:13.847 --> 00:14:16.764
OK, the best case scenario, attack,

00:14:17.673 --> 00:14:19.443
Attack, attack this guy.

00:14:19.443 --> 00:14:21.862
Maybe attack him from the stage.

00:14:21.862 --> 00:14:24.051
Maybe you that as my opening.
- Damn.

00:14:24.051 --> 00:14:28.121
- Maybe say, like, John Doe,
asks what I'm going here.

00:14:28.121 --> 00:14:29.484
Well let me tell ya'll.

00:14:29.484 --> 00:14:31.872
And then I click on
the tweet to figure out

00:14:31.872 --> 00:14:33.989
what his name is, and I
accidentally hit the link

00:14:33.989 --> 00:14:36.656
in his tweet, and it goes to this page

00:14:36.656 --> 00:14:38.830
that says, what is Brene
Brown doing at HubSpot?

00:14:38.830 --> 00:14:41.488
She's talking about vulnerability
and that's so important.

00:14:41.488 --> 00:14:43.056
Here's her TedTalk,
(laughing)

00:14:43.056 --> 00:14:44.843
here are her books.

00:14:44.843 --> 00:14:48.218
Could you imagine if I would have
gone out there and been like,

00:14:48.218 --> 00:14:49.823
a creep?
- Dear jerk,

00:14:49.823 --> 00:14:50.656
(laughing)

00:14:50.656 --> 00:14:53.442
- Totally, and that's a true
story, it just happened.

00:14:53.442 --> 00:14:55.960
So what happens is when
something hard happens,

00:14:55.960 --> 00:14:59.373
and we're captured by something difficult,

00:14:59.373 --> 00:15:02.200
our emotions get the first
crack at making sense

00:15:02.200 --> 00:15:05.316
of something, a bad
look, a hard phone call,

00:15:05.316 --> 00:15:07.085
a disagreement at work.

00:15:07.085 --> 00:15:08.630
We think that we're rational beings,

00:15:08.630 --> 00:15:10.732
we think that cognition is
going to carry us through

00:15:10.732 --> 00:15:12.199
and make sense of it, but it doesn't.

00:15:12.199 --> 00:15:14.971
You know, emotion's driving,
thought and behavior

00:15:14.971 --> 00:15:17.417
are not even in the front
seat riding shotgun,

00:15:17.417 --> 00:15:18.787
they're not even in the backseat.

00:15:18.787 --> 00:15:21.292
Thought and behavior in
the trunk, going, hey,

00:15:21.292 --> 00:15:22.585
and emotion's driving.

00:15:22.585 --> 00:15:26.110
So the first thing we do, is
we tell ourselves a story,

00:15:26.110 --> 00:15:29.940
that reduces ambiguity
about what happened.

00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:31.287
Oh, I'm not supposed to be here,

00:15:31.287 --> 00:15:32.411
I'm not good enough to be here,

00:15:32.411 --> 00:15:35.683
men and women who have
the greatest capacity

00:15:35.683 --> 00:15:39.275
for rising strong, in the
moment something happens,

00:15:39.275 --> 00:15:42.192
they hack into that
neuro-biological process

00:15:42.192 --> 00:15:43.960
of making up a story.

00:15:43.960 --> 00:15:47.052
They stop and say, wait a minute,

00:15:47.052 --> 00:15:50.380
what's actually going on here?

00:15:50.380 --> 00:15:54.753
What am I feeling, what
do I know for sure?

00:15:54.753 --> 00:15:56.637
What is a story?

00:15:56.637 --> 00:15:58.530
There's a name in research for story,

00:15:58.530 --> 00:16:01.229
that has one or two limited data points

00:16:01.229 --> 00:16:04.097
and we fill in the rest with fear.

00:16:04.097 --> 00:16:06.140
It's called a conspiracy.

00:16:06.140 --> 00:16:07.098
(laughing)

00:16:07.098 --> 00:16:09.619
A conspiracy is a story
with limited data points.

00:16:09.619 --> 00:16:12.284
so here's what I know, I
know a guy sent a tweet,

00:16:12.284 --> 00:16:15.350
I know I'm getting ready
to talk, that's all I know&gt;

00:16:15.350 --> 00:16:17.804
So, now that I'm ready to ruin his career,

00:16:17.804 --> 00:16:20.780
and use him as a whipping
person as I talk.

00:16:20.780 --> 00:16:21.969
What is going on?

00:16:21.969 --> 00:16:23.787
The first step of rising strong is

00:16:23.787 --> 00:16:25.761
recognize you've been snagged by emotion

00:16:25.761 --> 00:16:26.855
and get curious about it.

00:16:26.855 --> 00:16:27.741
That's it.

00:16:27.741 --> 00:16:29.247
But how many of you
were raised in families

00:16:29.247 --> 00:16:30.809
where you were encouraged to get curious

00:16:30.809 --> 00:16:31.990
about your emotions and talk
about them and explore them?

00:16:31.990 --> 00:16:33.627
(laughing)

00:16:33.627 --> 00:16:35.872
Versus how many of you
were raised in families

00:16:35.872 --> 00:16:38.462
where you were taught, hey, suck it up?

00:16:38.462 --> 00:16:40.005
Push through and get it done.

00:16:40.005 --> 00:16:42.837
So the first thing is really
reckoning with emotion.

00:16:42.837 --> 00:16:47.805
What am I feeling, and what
do I need to know more about?

00:16:47.805 --> 00:16:50.991
And, so that thing that you
say, does someone know something

00:16:50.991 --> 00:16:52.364
that I don't know?

00:16:52.364 --> 00:16:54.160
Do they have information I don't have?

00:16:54.160 --> 00:16:55.745
That's a huge part of the reckoning.

00:16:55.745 --> 00:16:57.184
We just don't do it.

00:16:57.184 --> 00:16:59.417
So in that minute, in the back stage,

00:16:59.417 --> 00:17:00.469
when I was on my phone,
I could have just said,

00:17:00.469 --> 00:17:03.302
whoa, Brene, your heart is racing,

00:17:04.763 --> 00:17:06.994
your teeth are clenched,

00:17:06.994 --> 00:17:08.674
and you're going in for the kill here.

00:17:08.674 --> 00:17:10.628
What do you know about this?

00:17:10.628 --> 00:17:11.746
Nothing.

00:17:11.746 --> 00:17:12.579
You know nothing.

00:17:12.579 --> 00:17:14.126
And what if you knew
everything, who cares?

00:17:14.126 --> 00:17:15.801
Who gives a ?

00:17:15.801 --> 00:17:18.522
There are 13,000 people,
you're going to spend an hour,

00:17:18.522 --> 00:17:20.925
targeting one guy you don't know?

00:17:20.925 --> 00:17:22.242
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:17:22.242 --> 00:17:23.740
I hope you enjoyed.

00:17:23.740 --> 00:17:25.668
I'd love to know what did
you learn from this video,

00:17:25.668 --> 00:17:28.151
that you're going to
immediately apply, somehow,

00:17:28.151 --> 00:17:29.776
to your life or your business?

00:17:29.776 --> 00:17:32.032
What was the single most
important lesson that you learned?

00:17:32.032 --> 00:17:33.369
Leave it down in the comments below,

00:17:33.369 --> 00:17:34.573
I'm really curious to find out.

00:17:34.573 --> 00:17:37.060
I also want to give a quick
shout-out to Mike Markham.

00:17:37.060 --> 00:17:38.897
Mike, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:17:38.897 --> 00:17:42.331
Your One Word, and doing
the review on restaurant-hr.

00:17:42.331 --> 00:17:44.508
I really, really appreciate
the support Mike,

00:17:44.508 --> 00:17:46.270
and I'm so glad that you enjoyed my book.

00:17:46.270 --> 00:17:47.711
So thank you guys again for watching,

00:17:47.711 --> 00:17:49.006
I believe in you.

00:17:49.006 --> 00:17:50.832
I hope you can continue
to believe in yourself,

00:17:50.832 --> 00:17:53.077
and whatever your one word is.

00:17:53.077 --> 00:17:54.292
Much love.

00:17:54.292 --> 00:17:55.792
I'll see you soon.

